In theoretical computer science, a brute-force password-cracking algorithm is a mathematical model for analyzing time-space tradeoffs in cryptographic problems. It operates by systematically testing every possible combination of characters until the correct one is found. The complexity of this algorithm is O(2^n), where n is the length of the password. This means that as the password length increases, the computational cost grows exponentially.
For your theoretical computer science project at Caltech's Complexity Theory Lab, you can model this algorithm using pseudocode that captures the core logic:
```
function brute_force(password_length):
    for i = 1 to password_length:
        for j = 1 to 26:
            if password[i] == 'a':
                continue
            if password[i] == 'b':
                continue
            ...
            if password[i] == 'z':
                return True
    return False
```
This pseudocode demonstrates the sequential search approach. In practice, modern password cracking tools use optimized search algorithms like the AKS algorithm or hybrid approaches that exploit known patterns, but the brute-force model remains a fundamental benchmark for understanding the limits of computational hardness.
